were erased. The notion of 【693†authentic】/【694†plagal】 modes (\"hypo-\" modes) was dropped, as the range of many pieces spans more than one octave. Additionally, Glarean's later modes, aeolian and ionian, were just as important as the original authentic church modes.\nL1098: ## Color notes\nL1099: \nL1100: As a listener, you may experience modes as sounding similar to major or minor, but with certain 【697†inflected】 notes—【688†color notes】.【698†^{[1]}】 Modes will sound major-ish or minor-ish based on the quality of the third above the tonic. The major-ish modes are 【699†mixolydian】 and 【700†lydian】; the minor-ish modes are 【701†aeolian】, 【702†dorian】, and 【703†phrygian】.\nL1101: Example 3 lists each mode, the quality of the third above the tonic, its color note that distinguishes it from major/natural minor, and its pitches parallel and relative to C major.\nL1102: Mode name  | Major tonic or minor tonic?  | Color note  | Mode, parallel to C major  | Mode, relative to C major\nL1103: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL1104: Ionian (major)  | Major  | n/a  | C D E F G A B C  | C D E F G A B C\nL1105: Dorian  | Minor  | la [latex](\\uparrow\\hat6)[/latex]  | C D E♭ F G A B♭ C  | D E F G A B C D\nL1106: Phrygian  | Minor  | ra [latex](\\downarrow\\hat2)[/latex]  | C D♭ E♭ F G A♭ B♭ C  | E F G A B C D E\nL1107: Lydian  | Major  | fi [latex](\\uparrow\\hat4)[/latex]  | C D E F♯ G A B C  | F G A B C D E F\nL1108: Mixolydian  | Major  | te [latex](\\downarrow\\hat7)[/latex]  | C D E F G A B♭ C  | G A B C D E F G\nL1109: Aeolian (natural minor)  | Minor  | te [latex](\\downarrow\\hat7)[/latex]—no raised leading tone  | C D E♭ F G A♭ B♭ C  | A B C D E F G A\nL1110: Example 3. Important characteristics of each mode.\nL1111: Example 4 provides a brief summary of this information. The modes are grouped by whether the tonic chord is major or minor, which helps with aural identification of modal passages (as discussed further below). The top line shows modes whose [latex]\\hat3[/latex] is a major third above tonic (do–mi): major, mixolydian, and lydian. The bottom line shows modes whose [latex]\\hat3[/latex] is a minor third above tonic (do–me): aeolian, dorian, and phrygian.\nL1112: The dotted slurs connect the distinctive 【688†color note】 of each mode with its major/minor counterpart.\nL1113: 【704†iframe†musescore.com】\nL1114: \nL1115: Example 4. Modes grouped by major vs. minor tonic, with color notes shown in blue.\nL1116: \nL1117: Notice that aeolian is identical to the natural minor scale. When discussing modality in the 20^{th} and 21^{st} centuries, it can be useful to distinguish between aeolian, which would avoid the raised leading tone (ti, [latex]\\uparrow\\hat7[/latex]) and instead consistently use te ([latex]\\downarrow\\hat7[/latex]).\nL1118: ## Hearing modal tonics\nL1119: \nL1120: Because many listeners are so enculturated to hear all music as being major or minor, 20^{th} and 21^{st} century composers who wish to create a modal sound will often spend extra energy on emphasizing the tonic pitch of their chosen mode. (Note that this may or may not involve emphasizing a tonic chord!) Following are several ways in which composers may create a sense of tonic in modal music.\nL1121:   * repeating the tonic pitch\nL1122:   * agogic accents (using longer note values) on the tonic pitch, including using a drone of the tonic pitch\nL1123:   * metrical accents on the tonic pitch\nL1124:   * using the tonic pitch as the lowest pitch\nL1125:   * cadencing on the tonic pitch\nL1126: ## Identifying modes\nL1127: \nL1128: If you are not used to playing in and listening to modes, it can be daunting to identify and distinguish modes. Here is a step-by-step process for distinguishing all the modes discussed here, illustrated as a flowchart in Example 5.\nL1129: \nL1130: Example 5. Flowchart illustrating the process of identifying modes.\nL1131: ### 1. Identify the quality of tonic.\nL1132: \nL1133: Listen for the tonic pitch. Then, listen for the whole tonic chord. Is the third of that chord major or minor? This distinguishes the major-ish modes (major, mixolydian, lydian) from the minor-ish modes (minor, aeolian, dorian).\nL1134: ### 2. Listen and look for [latex]\\hat{\\mathsf{7}}[/latex] (ti or te).\nL1135: \nL1136: Compare the [latex]\\hat{7}[/latex] to the leading tone (ti) a half step below tonic that we typically hear in minor and major pieces. If [latex]\\hat{7}[/latex] is a whole step below tonic (te), then it's lowered, which means that the piece is (at least temporarily) in a mode.\nL1137: \nL1138: If you heard a major tonic and [latex]\\hat{7}[/latex] is lowered (te), then you are in mixolydian.\nL1139: If you heard a minor tonic and [latex]\\hat{7}[/latex] is raised (ti), then you are in minor.\nL1140: \nL1141: If your mode is not already identified, proceed to step 3.\nL1142: ### 3. Listen and look for other color notes— fi ([latex]\\uparrow\\hat{\\mathsf{4}}[/latex]) in major, la ([latex]\\uparrow\\hat{\\mathsf{6}}[/latex]) in minor, or ra [latex](\\downarrow\\hat2)[/latex] in minor.\nL1143: \nL1144: If [latex]\\hat{7}[/latex] did not identify the mode for you, listen and look for other raised color notes.\nL1145: \nL1146: If [latex]\\hat{4}[/latex] is raised (fi) in a major-tonic mode, you are in lydian. If it is not, you are in major.\nL1147: If [latex]\\hat{2}[/latex] is lowered (ra) in a minor-tonic mode, you are in phrygian. If [latex]\\hat{6}[/latex] is raised (la) in a minor-tonic mode, you are in dorian. If neither [latex]\\hat{7}, \\hat6,[/latex] nor [latex]\\hat{2}[/latex] is altered (te, le, re), you are in aeolian.\nL1148: # Modes in a Global Context\nL1149: Almost all cultures in any place and time have had music, and the vast majority of them have organized pitch into what Western music theory considers types of modes. So, as you might imagine, there are a lot of options for composers prepared to look out beyond the Western tradition to explore. Around 1900, composers in Europe and North America began to do just that.\nL1150: Composers drew on music of the African diaspora (which would come to exert a huge influence over all forms of music making in the 20^{th} century), the ancient ragas of India, China, the Balkans, and many more besides. Clearly, there’s no way we can do justice to such a huge topic here—nor indeed could 20^{th}-century Western classical music, which often flattened out nuances of these complex systems. Following is an incomplete list of non-European sources of mode-like pitch collections:\nL1151:   * Indian raga\nL1152:   * Arabic maqam and Turkish makam\nL1153:   * Balinese pelog and slendro\nL1154:   * Jewish prayer modes\nL1155:   * Persian dastgah\nL1156:   * Japanese scales\nL1157: \nL1158: Each of these systems of pitch organization is related to the concept of mode discussed here, but each has nuances and practices that distinguish them from the diatonic modes, even if the pitches are identical.\nL1159: \nL1160: Further Reading\nL1161: \nL1162:   * Persichetti, Vincent. 1961. Twentieth-Century Harmony. New York: W. W. Norton.\nL1163: \nL1164: Assignments\nL1165:   1. Identifying modes (.【705†pdf】, .【706†mscz】). Asks students to identify 20^{th}-century modes versus major/minor, circle inflected pitches, and explain how a pitch center is articulated. Music examples are transcribed from the TV show Great British Bake Off (music by Tom Howe, © Accorder Music Publishing, used with permission). 【707†Worksheet playlist†open.spotify.com】\nL1166:   2. Additional beginner's worksheets can be found in 【708†Introduction to Diatonic Modes and the Chromatic \"Scale.\"】\nL1167: ## Media Attributions\nL1168: \nL1169:   * Identifying Modes flowchart with Phrygian © Sarah Louden and Megan Lavengood is licensed under a 【709†CC BY-SA (Attribution ShareAlike)†creativecommons.org】 license\nL1170: \nL1171: * * *\nL1172: \nL1173:   1. Vincent Persichetti (1961) referred to these notes as \"characteristic notes.\" 【710†↵】\nL1174: ## License\nL1175: \nL1176: 【711†Image: Icon for the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License】\nL1177: \nL1178: 【0†Open Music Theory】 Copyright © 2023 by Mark Gotham; Kyle Gullings; Chelsey Hamm; Bryn Hughes; Brian Jarvis; Megan Lavengood; and John Peterson is licensed under a 【709†Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org】, except where otherwise noted.\n"
